{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"REBAR AC-VAGUENESS DEFECT ISOLATED - it is the criterion, not the harness. Across five Wave 3 runs rebar's AC-vagueness criterion fired on terms appearing in narrative prose, Non-goals sections and appended third-party sub-issue bodies rather than on acceptance-criteria lines. Those runs all carried harness-appended criteria blocks, so the defect was partly attributable to scaffolding and was recorded with that caveat. The 4-arm carbon run removes the confound: the plan carries its own verbatim acceptance-criteria sections, nothing was appended, and every arm read identical bytes. The criterion STILL misfires - a judge recorded rebar item 15 reporting the term appropriate as vague when the word occurs exactly once in the plan, at line 280, in narrative prose belonging to no acceptance criterion. The compound-and ground on the same row stands, so the row is not a false positive, but the vagueness ground is contradicted. Conclusion: this is a genuine fault in the criterion's scoping, reproducing on a clean unscaffolded plan, and the earlier scaffolding caveat should be withdrawn rather than carried forward."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789446942090742001,"uuid":"64bd0b89-730e-4988-8c6a-ac9c0c15f65d"}